  "notes": "West leads the ♣K against 4♠. West wins the first trick and switches to diamonds. South must lose a trick in diamonds. The only possible problem is that 2 trump tricks may have to be lost.\n\nSouth should duck the diamond shift in an attempt to sever the link between the defenders' hands when diamonds are 5-2. The defense will continue diamonds, South winning the ♦A.\n\nIf either defender has 4 trumps including the ♠Q, it will be necessary to use the ♠10 to force out the ♠Q. South should lead a low spade out of his hand. West will win the ♠Q, but South can easily draw trump and take the balance.\n\nIf South had won the ♦A on the first round of the suit, he would fail. After winning the ♠Q, West would play his other diamond to East. A diamond return from East would promote a second trump trick for West.\n\nThe recommended line of play will fail when East has a singleton ♠Q and 5 diamonds. After winning the ♠Q a third round of diamonds will promote a second trump trick. If East has a singleton spade, however, it is 4 times more likely that his singleton is a small card than it is the ♠Q.",
